#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the gates a repository subscribes to.

The manifest is a plain text file, one gate per line:

    # .vibe-verifier
    no-duplicate-package-json-keys
    new-source-has-test --source 'lib/**/*.ts' --soak
    branch-name-length --max 37

The aggregate follows the gate contract: exit 2 if any gate could not run, else
exit 1 if any gate found violations, else 0. A missing or empty manifest is
exit 2: a run that verified nothing must not look like a pass. A gate whose pass
checked less than it could leaves a note (`qualify` in gates/_contract.py), and its
summary row reads `PASS (<note>)`.

On a pull request the manifest is judged from the base: the run's entries are the union of
the base's and the head's gates, and a gate in both takes the base's arguments. A PR that adds a
gate is bitten by it now; one that removes a gate, appends --soak or raises a limit is still
judged by the line it started from. The base is resolved the way the gates resolve theirs
(--base-ref, then $VIBE_VERIFIER_BASE_REF, $GITHUB_BASE_REF, main, master, HEAD~1); with no
base, or no manifest at the base, the head manifest stands alone (a first subscription, a
laptop with no history). The workflow file itself is still the head's, so this is a guard
against a PR quietly weakening its own manifest, not a platform guarantee. Every gate runs with
$VIBE_VERIFIER_MANIFEST set to the manifest file the run read (the head's): `repo-rules --doc`
lists the rules that manifest enables.

`consumers` is the inventory and drift check for subscribed repositories. It reads
each repository's manifest, its workflow stub, and every other workflow under
.github/workflows/ that pins a catalog action (a harness copy such as qae-explore.yml)
through `gh api` (read-only) and reports, per repository: whether each pinned commit is
current, stale (a commit since it touched gates/, bin/, actions/, consumer/, tools/ or rules/), or unknown
to this repository's history; whether the stub matches consumer/vibe-verifier.yml outside
the pin line and the `runs-on:` values (each job's runner is the consumer's choice, and a pin bump
keeps it); whether every gate in the manifest exists; and the native enforcement GitHub
holds for the repository (SHA pinning, the actions allowlist, Dependabot alerts), which no
workflow token can read, so this inventory is where a revert to `all` surfaces. The source
repository gets a row too, with its stub and manifest checks skipped: it carries neither by
design, and its native state is watched like any consumer's. Exit 2 if any repository could
not be read, 1 if anything needs action, else 0.

With `--wrapper PATH` (a checkout of an organization's CI repository that subscribes its
repositories to the catalog) the inventory has three more kinds of pin. The wrapper gets a row of
kind `wrapper`: no stub is expected, every catalog pin in its workflows is judged like a
consumer's, and a review.yml copy is compared under `jobs:` only, with the token secret named
alike and its verify step's gate list left out (the wrapper owns its header, its secret and that
list). Every ruleset of the wrapper's organization whose `workflows` rule requires a wrapper
workflow gets a row: each pinned commit is current or stale against the wrapper's --wrapper-ref
(stale when a commit since it touched that workflow), a bare ref is unpinned, and a ruleset that is
not active needs action. The repositories it targets by name are subscribers with no stub
(`stub: ruleset`) and no manifest of their own: their gate list is the wrapper's entry for them,
repos/<name>/vibe-verifier in the wrapper at --wrapper-ref, which must exist, and a .vibe-verifier
or .vibe-verifier-review left in such a repository is stray, since nothing reads it. The wrapper's
own row is judged the same way: its own pull requests run its gate workflow. A `uses:` of a
wrapper workflow in any repository's workflows is judged the same way, so a caller of a legacy
reusable workflow is listed even with no manifest.

`apply-down` bumps stale pins, and nothing else. It is two-stage on purpose: with no
`--confirm` it reads the same inventory and prints a plan plus a digest of exactly what
it would write; `--confirm <digest>` opens one pull request per repository, changing only
pin lines (one commit per workflow file). It never merges, never touches a repository whose
stub has drifted, and never opens a second pull request for a bump that is already open. A
plan whose digest no longer matches is refused rather than applied, so a bump is reviewed as
the thing it was. With `--wrapper PATH` it bumps both levels in the same plan: the wrapper's
catalog pins in a pull request to the wrapper, each caller's pins of wrapper workflows in a pull
request of its own, and each ruleset's pins with a PUT of that ruleset's rules, where only the
stale entries' `sha` moves to the wrapper's head. A ruleset moves to what is on the wrapper's
--wrapper-ref, so run it again once the wrapper's own bump has merged.

`tool <name>` prints the directory where the node toolchain pinned by tools/<name>/package-lock.json
is installed (installing it first when the cache does not have that lockfile's digest), so a
workflow step can run a pinned toolchain the same way a gate does. Exit 2 when there is no such
toolchain or the install fails.

`criteria <file> [--changed-files FILE]` reads a PR body with the acceptance-verdict grammar: its
criteria are the list under its `Acceptance criteria` heading, and a body without that heading lists
none. Its first line is `none: <reason>` when it needs no browser check, else `criteria: <n>`. It needs none when it declares `- None: <why>`, or, given the pull request's
changed paths one per line, when it lists no criteria and every path is CI configuration or
documentation no site renders. A harness uses it to skip work, so the decision is made by the same
code in the job that explores and the job that judges. After `criteria: <n>` it prints one line per
annotation a criterion carries (`AC2 references: home-desktop`, `AC3 roles: admin, read-only`,
`AC4 malformed: [ref: ]`), read the way the acceptance-verdict gate reads them. With `--ticket FILE`,
the criteria of the ticket the pull request implements (written by the consumer's workflow) count too,
as TC1, TC2, ...: a ticket that lists criteria, or a non-empty one in which none can be found (printed
as `ticket: ...`, and refused by the gate), makes a check needed whatever the pull request declares, so
it cannot drop its ticket's requirements; an empty file links no ticket. With `--max-shards N` it then
prints `shards: [1,2,...]`, the explorers the criteria are split across, for a harness to run in parallel:
one walk is a criterion as one role it names at one width, an explorer is sized for six, and there are
never more than N explorers nor than criteria (a criterion is never split). A `walks: <n>` line says how
many it counted. Which criteria each explorer walks is `qae-inputs --shard`'s to say: it also knows the
feature re-walk, which this command does not. The widths are the `--widths` of the qae-artifacts
line of `--manifest FILE`, judged from the base like a run (no manifest counts one width). More walks
than the explorers are sized for prints one `::warning::` with the numbers. Exit 2 when a file cannot be
read.

`qae-inputs [--manifest FILE] [--widths-file FILE] [--references DIR] [--evidence DIR] [--listing FILE]
[--digests FILE] [--turns FILE [--criteria FILE] [--ticket FILE] [--features DIR] [--shard K --shards N]
[--share FILE]]` prepares what the QAE
explorer and its gates read from the site step's supplies and the QAE manifest, before the explorer runs.
Each design reference `<key>.png` the site step wrote to DIR (default qae-inputs/references) is copied
to `<evidence>/references/<key>.png` (default qae-artifacts), listed with its size in FILE (default
qae-inputs/references.md) for the explorer, and its sha256 written to the digests file as one JSON object,
which the workflow declares as an output so the gate judges the image the workflow supplied and never one
the explorer wrote. Given the manifest, the `--widths` of its qae-artifacts line (judged from the base,
like a run) are written one per line to the widths file (default qae-inputs/widths), so the explorer
checks each criterion at the widths the gate will require; no such option writes no file. With
`--turns FILE` it also writes the explorer's turn cap there, sized to its walks: each criterion of the PR
body (`--criteria`, default qae-inputs/pr-body.md) and of the ticket (`--ticket`, default
qae-inputs/ticket.md; absent is none) once per role it names and per width, plus each feature to re-walk
(`--features`, default qae-inputs/features), which is one walk per three states the manifest's
acceptance-verdict line lets the explorer walk of it (`--max-states`, default 3). With `--shard K --shards N`
the explorer is one of N that share the criteria (`criteria --max-shards` decided N). In document order each
criterion goes whole to the explorer with the fewest walks so far, the lowest-numbered on a tie, and the first
explorer starts with the walks of the feature re-walk, which only it does, so it takes fewer criteria and
reaches the re-walk in its time. Every explorer counts the features the same way, to make the same
assignment, and for one past the first `--features DIR` and its listing are then removed: it is not handed
them. The cap is sized to the explorer's own share, and `--share FILE` gets the one sentence that names that
share for its prompt (an empty line for one explorer). It prints `references: <n>`, one
`<key>: <width>x<height>` line each, `widths: <list>` (or `widths: none`) and, with `--turns`,
`max turns: <n> (<walks> walks)` and, for one explorer of several, `share: <ids> (explorer K of N)`. Exit 2
when a supplied file is not a PNG or its name is not a reference key, the manifest or the criteria file
cannot be read, or the explorer has nothing to walk (the criteria changed after they were counted).

`features --manifest FILE --changed-files FILE [--out DIR [--listing FILE]]` reads the manifest's
acceptance-verdict line (judged from the base, like a run) and, when it has `--features`, prints which
features of that feature map the changed paths touch: the same selection the gate requires a
`regression-check:` line for (gates/_features.py). Its first line is `features: <n>`; with `--out` it
copies each selected feature file, as the working tree has it (the pull request's own copy, so one that
updates a feature is re-walked against its updated Reach and Verify), to `<DIR>/<id>.md` for a QAE
explorer to read, and with `--listing` writes the list the explorer reads beside them: the most states to
walk of each feature (the line's `--max-states`, default 3) and the changed paths that selected each. A
line without `--features` prints `features: 0` and the reason. Exit 2 when the manifest, the changed
paths or the map cannot be read.

`rules-doc` renders the Markdown block that lists every rule the manifest's `repo-rules` lines enable
(their packs and rule directories, read from the working tree): each rule's id, whether it blocks or
is advisory (as the gate judges it), its language and globs, and its message, sorted by id, between
BEGIN and END comment lines that say it is generated. It prints the block, or with `--write PATH`
replaces the block in that file, appending it when the file has none. `repo-rules --doc PATH` fails a
pull request whose block differs from what this renders for its HEAD.

`qa-review` posts the QAE's one review comment on a pull request, or edits it in place when it is
already there: passed, changes needed, not required, or could not run, with the head it judged,
each criterion's verdict from the explorer (and each re-walked feature's), and a link to the run. The state comes from what the
workflow's jobs and gates concluded, never from the explorer's prose.
"""
import argparse
import base64
import copy
import fnmatch
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
GATES = os.path.join(ROOT, "gates")
sys.path.insert(0, GATES)
from _acceptance import (REFERENCE_KEY, annotations, body_criteria as read_criteria, check_lines,  # noqa: E402
                         declares_none, not_required, png_size, said, ticket_items, verdict)
from _contract import QUALIFIER, CannotRun, resolve_base  # noqa: E402
from _features import add_selection_arguments, describe, selection  # noqa: E402
from _tools import NODE_TOOLS, ensure, ensure_node_tool  # noqa: E402
import qae_artifacts  # noqa: E402
import repo_rules  # noqa: E402
LABEL = {0: "PASS", 1: "FAIL", 2: "COULD NOT RUN"}
STUB_PATH = ".github/workflows/vibe-verifier.yml"
STUB_TEMPLATE = os.path.join(ROOT, "consumer", "vibe-verifier.yml")
WORKFLOWS_DIR = ".github/workflows"
# Any catalog action pinned in a workflow: the stub's gates@, a harness copy's gates@ and criteria@.
PIN_LINE = re.compile(r"(vibe-verifier/actions/[A-Za-z0-9_-]+@)([^\s#]+)([^\n]*)")
# The value of a job's `runs-on:` line (a label or a flow list) is the consumer's choice of runner.
# It stops at any character YAML reads as a line break, so nothing after it hides on the same line.
RUNS_ON = re.compile("^([ \t]*runs-on:)[^\n\r\x85\u2028\u2029]*", re.MULTILINE)
RELEASE_PATHS = ["gates", "bin", "actions", "consumer", "tools", "rules"]  # a commit under these makes every older pin stale


def available():
    return sorted(name[:-3].replace("_", "-") for name in os.listdir(GATES)
                  if name.endswith(".py") and not name.startswith("_"))


def parse_manifest(path):
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        sys.exit(fail("no manifest at %s" % path))
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        entries = parse_entries(handle.read(), path)
    if not entries:
        sys.exit(fail("%s subscribes to no gates" % path))
    return entries


def parse_entries(text, origin):
    """[(gate, [args])] from manifest text; a line shlex cannot read is exit 2."""
    entries = []
    for number, raw in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        try:
            words = shlex.split(raw, comments=True)
        except ValueError as error:
            sys.exit(fail("%s line %d: %s" % (origin, number, error)))
        if words:
            entries.append((words[0], words[1:]))
    return entries


def base_manifest(args):
    """(base ref, [(gate, [args])]) for the manifest at the base, or (None, None) when there is
    no base to resolve, the manifest lies outside the repository, or the base has no manifest."""
    try:
        base = resolve_base(args.repo, args.base_ref)
    except CannotRun:
        return None, None
    relative = os.path.relpath(os.path.abspath(args.manifest), os.path.abspath(args.repo))
    if relative.startswith(".."):
        return None, None
    shown = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", args.repo, "show", "%s:%s" % (base, relative.replace(os.sep, "/"))],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
    if shown.returncode != 0:
        return None, None
    return base, parse_entries(shown.stdout, "%s:%s" % (base, relative))


def judged_entries(args, notice=None):
    """The entries the run judges: the head manifest, unioned with the base's on a pull request. Where they
    differ it says so on `notice` (default stdout)."""
    head = parse_manifest(args.manifest)
    base, at_base = base_manifest(args)
    if not at_base:
        return head
    base_args = dict(at_base)
    merged = [(gate, base_args.get(gate, gate_args)) for gate, gate_args in head]
    merged += [(gate, gate_args) for gate, gate_args in at_base if gate not in dict(head)]
    if merged != head:
        print("vibe-verifier: judged by the manifest at %s where it differs from the head's "
              "(a gate this branch removed or weakened still runs as it was; a gate it added runs too)" % base,
              file=notice or sys.stdout, flush=True)
    return merged


def fail(message):
    print("vibe-verifier: " + message, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def run(args):
    entries = judged_entries(args)
    known = available()
    results = []
    for gate, gate_args in entries:
        if not re.match(r"^[a-z0-9-]+$", gate) or gate not in known:
            print("vibe-verifier: unknown gate %r (available: %s)" % (gate, ", ".join(known)), file=sys.stderr)
            results.append((gate, gate_args, 2, ""))
            continue
        command = [sys.executable, os.path.join(GATES, gate.replace("-", "_") + ".py"),
                   "--repo", args.repo, "--format", args.format]
        if args.base_ref:
            command += ["--base-ref", args.base_ref]
        print("::group::%s" % gate if args.format == "github" else "== %s ==" % gate, flush=True)
        code, note = gate_process(command + gate_args, os.path.abspath(args.manifest))
        if args.format == "github":
            print("::endgroup::", flush=True)
        results.append((gate, gate_args, code if code in (0, 1) else 2, note))
    rows = [(gate, LABEL[code] + (" (soak)" if code == 0 and "--soak" in gate_args else "")
             + (" (%s)" % note if code == 0 and note else "")) for gate, gate_args, code, note in results]
    print("\nvibe-verifier summary")
    for gate, status in rows:
        print("  %-36s %s" % (gate, status))
    summary = os.environ.get("GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY")
    if summary:
        with open(summary, "a", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write("### Vibe Verifier\n\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n")
            handle.writelines("| `%s` | %s |\n" % row for row in rows)
    codes = [code for _, _, code, _ in results]
    return 2 if 2 in codes else (1 if 1 in codes else 0)


def gate_process(command, manifest):
    """Run one gate: its exit code, and the note it left when its pass checked less than it could
    (`qualify` in gates/_contract.py), else an empty string."""
    handle, note_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="vv-note-")
    os.close(handle)
    try:
        code = subprocess.run(command, env=dict(os.environ, **{repo_rules.MANIFEST: manifest, QUALIFIER: note_path})).returncode
        with open(note_path, encoding="utf-8") as note:
            return code, note.read().strip()
    finally:
        os.unlink(note_path)


class CannotRead(Exception):
    """A read through gh failed for a reason other than the file not existing."""


def gh_file(repo, path):
    """The decoded file at `path` on the repository's default branch, or None when absent."""
    result = subprocess.run(["gh", "api", "repos/%s/contents/%s" % (repo, path), "--jq", ".content"],
                            capture_output=True, text=True)
    if result.returncode == 0:
        return base64.b64decode(result.stdout).decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    if "HTTP 404" in result.stderr:
        return None
    raise CannotRead((result.stderr.strip() or "gh api failed").splitlines()[-1])


REVIEW_HARNESS = os.path.join(ROOT, "harnesses", "review", "review.yml")
# The token line of a review harness copy; a wrapper names its own secret.
TOKEN_SECRET = re.compile(r"(claude_code_oauth_token: \$\{\{ secrets\.)[A-Za-z0-9_]+( \}\})")
# The one input a gates step takes its gate list from; a wrapper passes the list itself (`entries:`),
# because the repositories it runs in carry no manifest.
GATE_LIST_INPUT = re.compile(r"(vibe-verifier/actions/gates@[^\n]*\n\s+with:\n\s+)(?:manifest|entries): [^\n]*")
# A wrapper's gate list for one repository of its organization, by name, in the wrapper's checkout.
ENTRY = "repos/%s/vibe-verifier"
# What a repository subscribed through a wrapper must not carry: nothing reads it there.
MANIFESTS = (".vibe-verifier", ".vibe-verifier-review")
SOURCE = "source"  # this repository's own row: it carries no stub and its manifest is its own
WRAPPER = "wrapper"  # the wrapper's own row: it pins catalog actions for an organization and carries no stub
RULESET = "ruleset"  # a repository an organization ruleset subscribes through the wrapper: no stub expected
SETTLED = ("current", "ok", SOURCE, WRAPPER, RULESET)


def workflow_texts(repo):
    """{path: text} for every workflow file under .github/workflows/, the stub included."""
    try:
        entries = gh_json("repos/%s/contents/%s" % (repo, WORKFLOWS_DIR))
    except CannotRead as error:
        if "HTTP 404" in str(error):
            return {}
        raise
    return {entry["path"]: gh_file(repo, entry["path"]) or "" for entry in entries
            if entry.get("type") == "file" and entry.get("path", "").endswith((".yml", ".yaml"))}


def workflow_pins(texts, with_stub=False):
    """{workflow path: ([pinned SHAs], text)} for every workflow that pins a catalog action. A harness
    copy (qae-explore.yml) pins gates@ and criteria@. The stub is left out unless asked for: a
    consumer's stub is judged on its own line of the row, and a wrapper's gate workflow is not a stub."""
    pins = {}
    for path, text in texts.items():
        found = [match.group(2) for match in PIN_LINE.finditer(text)]
        if found and (with_stub or path != STUB_PATH):
            pins[path] = (found, text)
    return pins


def jobs_of(text):
    """The workflow from its `jobs:` key on."""
    at = text.find("\njobs:\n")
    return text[at:] if at >= 0 else text


def comparable(text):
    """A copy of a catalog workflow with what its consumer owns blanked: each catalog pin and each
    `runs-on:` value. The `runs-on:` lines themselves still count, so a copy with more or fewer of
    them differs; `apply-down` rewrites only pin lines, so a bump keeps every runner."""
    return RUNS_ON.sub(r"\1 RUNNER", PIN_LINE.sub(r"\1SHA", text))


def harness_drift(path, text, wrapper=False):
    """What a harness copy changed outside its pin lines and `runs-on:` values, or None. Only review.yml
    is compared: every other line of it is the catalog's, so a copy that differs is running a review
    nobody released (qae-explore.yml has a consumer-owned build block and is not compared). A
    wrapper's copy owns its header (an organization ruleset requires it, and GitHub forbids a ruleset
    workflow cancel-in-progress, so it has no concurrency group), the name of its token secret, and
    the one line its verify step takes its gate list from (its repositories carry no
    .vibe-verifier-review, so it passes `entries:`): only its jobs are compared, with the secret named
    alike and that line left out."""
    if os.path.basename(path) != "review.yml":
        return None
    with open(REVIEW_HARNESS, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        template = handle.read()
    if wrapper:
        text, template = (GATE_LIST_INPUT.sub(r"\1GATE-LIST", TOKEN_SECRET.sub(r"\1TOKEN\2", jobs_of(body)))
                          for body in (text, template))
    if comparable(text) == comparable(template):
        return None
    return "drifts from harnesses/review/review.yml %s" % (
        "under jobs: outside the pin, token and gate-list lines" if wrapper else "outside the pin line")


def owner_repos(owner):
    result = subprocess.run(["gh", "repo", "list", owner, "--limit", "200", "--no-archived", "--json", "nameWithOwner",
                             "--jq", ".[].nameWithOwner"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise CannotRead((result.stderr.strip() or "gh repo list failed").splitlines()[-1])
    return sorted(result.stdout.split())


def git_in(checkout, *git_args):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", checkout, *git_args], capture_output=True, text=True)


def source_git(args, *git_args):
    return git_in(args.source, *git_args)


def origin_repo(checkout):
    """OWNER/NAME of a checkout's origin remote, or None."""
    url = git_in(checkout, "remote", "get-url", "origin").stdout.strip()
    match = re.search(r"[:/]([^/:]+/[^/]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$", url)
    return match.group(1) if match else None


def source_repo(args):
    """OWNER/NAME of the source checkout's origin, or None. Discovery skips it: the source
    repository dogfoods through its own ci.yml and never carries the consumer stub."""
    return origin_repo(args.source)


def commit_status(checkout, ref, sha, paths, noun):
    """current | stale (N <noun>s behind) | unknown | unpinned: a pin of `sha` judged against `ref`
    in `checkout`, stale when a commit since it touched `paths`."""
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{40}", sha):
        return "unpinned (%s is not a commit SHA)" % sha
    if git_in(checkout, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", sha, ref).returncode != 0:
        return "unknown (not in %s)" % ref
    behind = git_in(checkout, "rev-list", "--count", "%s..%s" % (sha, ref), "--", *paths).stdout.strip()
    return "current" if behind == "0" else "stale (%s %s%s behind)" % (behind, noun, "" if behind == "1" else "s")


def pin_status(args, sha):
    """A catalog pin, judged against the source history: stale when a release commit came since."""
    return commit_status(args.source, args.source_ref, sha, RELEASE_PATHS, "release commit")


class Wrapper:
    """A repository that subscribes an organization's repositories to the catalog on their behalf.
    Its own workflows pin catalog actions; the organization's rulesets require its workflows, pinned
    by commit, and a caller may still name one in a `uses:` line. `checkout` is a local clone judged
    at `ref`, the way --source is for the catalog."""

    def __init__(self, checkout, ref):
        self.checkout, self.ref = checkout, ref
        self.repo = origin_repo(checkout)
        self.owner = (self.repo or "/").split("/")[0]
        # A caller's pin of one of its workflows: OWNER/NAME/.github/workflows/<file>@<ref>
        self.call = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.-])(%s/(\.github/workflows/[^@\s]+)@)([^\s#]+)([^\n]*)"
                               % re.escape(self.repo or "-"), re.IGNORECASE)

    def status(self, sha, path):
        """A pin of one wrapper workflow: stale when a commit since it touched that workflow."""
        return commit_status(self.checkout, self.ref, sha, [path], "commit")

    def head(self):
        """(commit, date) of the ref every wrapper pin is judged against and bumped to."""
        sha = git_in(self.checkout, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", self.ref).stdout.strip()
        return sha, git_in(self.checkout, "log", "-1", "--date=short", "--format=%cd", sha).stdout.strip()

    def entry(self, repo):
        """(path, text or None): the gate list the wrapper carries for OWNER/NAME at its ref."""
        path = ENTRY % repo.split("/", 1)[1]
        shown = git_in(self.checkout, "show", "%s:%s" % (self.ref, path))
        return path, shown.stdout if shown.returncode == 0 else None


def load_wrapper(args):
    """(Wrapper, None), (None, why) when --wrapper is not a usable checkout, or (None, None) without it."""
    if not args.wrapper:
        return None, None
    wrapper = Wrapper(args.wrapper, args.wrapper_ref)
    if not wrapper.repo:
        return None, "--wrapper %s is not a checkout with an origin remote" % args.wrapper
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{40}", wrapper.head()[0]):
        return None, "ref %s not found in %s" % (args.wrapper_ref, args.wrapper)
    return wrapper, None


def wrapper_calls(wrapper, texts):
    """[(workflow path, called wrapper workflow path, ref)] for every `uses:` of a wrapper workflow."""
    return [(path, match.group(2), match.group(3)) for path, text in sorted(texts.items())
            for match in wrapper.call.finditer(text)]


def required_workflows(rules):
    """Every workflow entry of the `workflows` rules in `rules` (the live objects, so a bump edits them)."""
    return [entry for rule in rules if rule.get("type") == "workflows"
            for entry in (rule.get("parameters") or {}).get("workflows") or []]


def wrapper_rulesets(wrapper):
    """(the wrapper's repository id, [(ruleset, [(workflow path, pinned sha or ref)])]) for every ruleset
    of the wrapper's organization whose `workflows` rule requires a wrapper workflow. A user account
    has no organization rulesets (HTTP 404): an empty list, not an error."""
    wrapper_id = gh_json("repos/%s" % wrapper.repo).get("id")
    try:
        listed = gh_json("orgs/%s/rulesets?per_page=100" % wrapper.owner)
    except CannotRead as error:
        if "HTTP 404" in str(error):
            return wrapper_id, []
        raise
    found = []
    for summary in listed:
        ruleset = gh_json("orgs/%s/rulesets/%s" % (wrapper.owner, summary["id"]))
        pins = [(entry.get("path", ""), entry.get("sha") or entry.get("ref") or "")
                for entry in required_workflows(ruleset.get("rules") or []) if entry.get("repository_id") == wrapper_id]
        if pins:
            found.append((ruleset, pins))
    return wrapper_id, found


def ruleset_targets(ruleset, repos):
    """The OWNER/NAME repositories among `repos` a ruleset targets by name, or None when its
    conditions target another way (a repository id list, a custom property), which is not resolved."""
    conditions = ruleset.get("conditions") or {}
    by_name = conditions.get("repository_name")
    if by_name is None or set(conditions) - {"ref_name", "repository_name"}:
        return None

    def hit(name, patterns):
        return any(pattern == "~ALL" or fnmatch.fnmatchcase(name.lower(), pattern.lower()) for pattern in patterns)
    return sorted(repo for repo in repos if hit(repo.split("/", 1)[1], by_name.get("include") or [])
                  and not hit(repo.split("/", 1)[1], by_name.get("exclude") or []))


def ruleset_rows(args, wrapper, discovered):
    """([(ruleset name, enforcement, targets or None, [(workflow, status)])], {target: [ruleset names]})."""
    _, found = wrapper_rulesets(wrapper)
    if not found:
        return [], {}
    same_owner = args.owner and args.owner.lower() == wrapper.owner.lower()
    repos = discovered if same_owner else set(owner_repos(wrapper.owner))
    rows, targeted = [], {}
    for ruleset, pins in found:
        name = "%s/%s" % (wrapper.owner, ruleset.get("name"))
        targets = ruleset_targets(ruleset, repos)
        for repo in targets or []:
            targeted.setdefault(repo, []).append(name)
        rows.append((name, ruleset.get("enforcement") or "unset", targets,
                     [(os.path.basename(path), wrapper.status(sha, path)) for path, sha in pins]))
    return rows, targeted


def stub_status(stub):
    """ok when the stub equals the template outside the pin line and `runs-on:` values; otherwise what differs."""
    if stub is None:
        return "missing", None
    pins = PIN_LINE.findall(stub)
    if len(pins) != 1:
        return "drift (%d pin lines, expected 1)" % len(pins), None
    with open(STUB_TEMPLATE, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        template = handle.read()
    if comparable(stub) != comparable(template):
        return "drift (differs from consumer/vibe-verifier.yml outside the pin line)", pins[0][1]
    return "ok", pins[0][1]


def manifest_status(manifest):
    if manifest is None:
        return "missing", []
    gates = [shlex.split(line, comments=True) for line in manifest.splitlines()]
    gates = [words[0] for words in gates if words]
    if not gates:
        return "empty", []
    unknown = sorted(set(gates) - set(available()))
    return ("unknown gate %s" % ", ".join(unknown)) if unknown else "ok", gates


def wrapped_status(wrapper, repo, manifest):
    """(state, gates) for a repository the wrapper subscribes, the wrapper included: its gate list is
    the wrapper's entry for it. A manifest left in the repository (`manifest` is its .vibe-verifier)
    is read by nothing, so it is reported as stray rather than trusted."""
    path, text = wrapper.entry(repo)
    state, gates = manifest_status(text)
    problems = [] if state == "ok" else ["no %s in %s" % (path, wrapper.repo) if text is None else state]
    stray = [name for name in MANIFESTS if (manifest if name == ".vibe-verifier" else gh_file(repo, name)) is not None]
    if stray:
        problems.append("stray %s (the entry in %s is what runs)" % (" ".join(stray), wrapper.repo))
    return "; ".join(problems) or "ok", gates


def worst_status(args, shas):
    """One status for a file that may pin the same commit twice or two different ones."""
    statuses = [pin_status(args, sha) for sha in sorted(set(shas))]
    return "current" if all(s == "current" for s in statuses) else next(s for s in statuses if s != "current")


def catalog_rows(args, texts, wrapper=False):
    """[(workflow, status)] for every workflow pinning a catalog action, a review copy's drift included."""
    return [(os.path.basename(path), " ".join(filter(None, (worst_status(args, shas), harness_drift(path, text, wrapper)))))
            for path, (shas, text) in sorted(workflow_pins(texts, with_stub=wrapper).items())]


def native_status(repo):
    """(text, [problems]) for the enforcement GitHub itself holds: `sha_pinning_required`, the
    actions allowlist, and Dependabot alerts. Reading them needs an admin token, which a workflow's
    GITHUB_TOKEN is not, so drift back to `all` shows up here or nowhere.

    The pattern list is printed for review, never judged: GitHub holds the only copy of it and this
    tool carries no second one to compare against, so a widened list is visible in the row and does
    not flag. Same-owner actions are allowed by rule and need no pattern."""
    permissions = gh_json("repos/%s/actions/permissions" % repo)
    allowed = permissions.get("allowed_actions") or "unset"
    parts, problems = [], []
    if permissions.get("sha_pinning_required") is True:
        parts.append("sha-pin on")
    else:
        parts.append("sha-pin OFF")
        problems.append("sha_pinning_required is off")
    if allowed == "selected":
        patterns = gh_json("repos/%s/actions/permissions/selected-actions" % repo).get("patterns_allowed") or []
        parts.append("actions selected [%s]" % (" ".join(patterns) or "github-owned and same-owner only"))
    else:
        parts.append("actions %s" % allowed)
        problems.append("allowed_actions is %s, not selected" % allowed)
    if gh_ok("repos/%s/vulnerability-alerts" % repo):
        parts.append("alerts on")
    else:
        parts.append("alerts OFF")
        problems.append("Dependabot alerts are off")
    return ", ".join(parts), problems


def repo_row(args, repo, source, wrapper, targeted):
    """One inventory row, or None for a discovered repository that subscribes to nothing."""
    if repo == source:
        # The source repository subscribes through its own ci.yml, so only its native state
        # is judged; it is listed so every repository the catalog governs is watched.
        return (repo, "", SOURCE, SOURCE, [], [], native_status(repo))
    manifest, stub = gh_file(repo, ".vibe-verifier"), gh_file(repo, STUB_PATH)
    subscribed = manifest is not None or stub is not None or repo in targeted
    texts = workflow_texts(repo) if subscribed or wrapper else {}
    calls = [("%s (%s)" % (os.path.basename(path), wrapper.repo), wrapper.status(ref, called))
             for path, called, ref in wrapper_calls(wrapper, texts)] if wrapper else []
    if wrapper and repo == wrapper.repo:
        # The wrapper pins catalog actions in its own workflows, the gate workflow included, and
        # carries no stub. Its own pull requests run its gate workflow, so it needs an entry too.
        manifest_state, gates = wrapped_status(wrapper, repo, manifest)
        return (repo, "", WRAPPER, manifest_state, gates, catalog_rows(args, texts, wrapper=True), native_status(repo))
    if not subscribed:
        if calls:  # a caller of a wrapper workflow that subscribes to nothing itself: its pins are the row
            return (repo, "", "", "", [], calls, ("", []))
        if args.owner and repo not in (args.repo or []):
            return None  # discovery: an owner's repo that never subscribed is not a finding
        return (repo, "not subscribed", "missing", "missing", [], [], ("", []))
    if stub is None and repo in targeted:
        manifest_state, gates = wrapped_status(wrapper, repo, manifest)
        return (repo, "", RULESET, manifest_state, gates, catalog_rows(args, texts) + calls, native_status(repo))
    stub_state, sha = stub_status(stub)
    manifest_state, gates = manifest_status(manifest)
    return (repo, pin_status(args, sha) if sha else "", stub_state, manifest_state, gates,
            catalog_rows(args, texts) + calls, native_status(repo))


def consumers(args):
    source = source_repo(args)
    wrapper, error = load_wrapper(args)
    if error:
        return fail(error)
    try:
        discovered = set(owner_repos(args.owner)) if args.owner else set()
        rulesets, targeted = ruleset_rows(args, wrapper, discovered) if wrapper else ([], {})
    except CannotRead as error:
        return fail(str(error))
    repos = sorted(set(args.repo or []) | discovered | set(targeted) | ({wrapper.repo} if wrapper else set()))
    if not repos:
        return fail("no repositories given (use --repo OWNER/NAME or --owner OWNER)")
    if source_git(args, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", args.source_ref).returncode != 0:
        return fail("source ref %s not found in %s" % (args.source_ref, args.source))
    rows, unreadable = [], 0
    for repo in repos:
        try:
            row = repo_row(args, repo, source, wrapper, targeted)
        except CannotRead as error:
            row = (repo, "could not read: %s" % error, "", "", [], [], ("", []))
            unreadable += 1
        if row:
            rows.append(row)
    needs_action = 0
    for repo, pin, stub_state, manifest_state, gates, workflows, (native, native_problems) in rows:
        problems = [text for text in (pin, stub_state, manifest_state) if text and text not in SETTLED]
        problems += ["%s %s" % (name, status) for name, status in workflows if status != "current"]
        problems += native_problems
        needs_action += report(args, repo, problems)
        print("%-32s pin: %-44s stub: %-12s manifest: %s%s%s%s" % (
            repo, pin or "-", stub_state or "-", manifest_state or "-",
            "  [%s]" % " ".join(gates) if gates else "",
            "  workflows: %s" % ", ".join("%s %s" % pair for pair in workflows) if workflows else "",
            "  native: %s" % native if native else ""))
    for name, enforcement, targets, pins in rulesets:
        # A ruleset is where an organization pins the wrapper: one row, however many repositories it targets.
        problems = ["%s %s" % pair for pair in pins if pair[1] != "current"]
        problems += [] if enforcement == "active" else ["enforcement is %s, not active" % enforcement]
        problems += [] if targets is not None else ["targets repositories by something other than their names"]
        needs_action += report(args, "ruleset " + name, problems)
        print("%-32s enforcement: %s  targets: %s  pins: %s" % (
            "ruleset " + name, enforcement,
            "[%s]" % " ".join(repo.split("/", 1)[1] for repo in targets) if targets is not None else "not by name",
            ", ".join("%s %s" % pair for pair in pins)))
    print("\n%d repositor%s%s: %d current, %d need action, %d could not be read" % (
        len(rows), "y" if len(rows) == 1 else "ies",
        " and %d ruleset%s" % (len(rulesets), "" if len(rulesets) == 1 else "s") if rulesets else "",
        len(rows) + len(rulesets) - needs_action, needs_action - unreadable, unreadable))
    return 2 if unreadable else (1 if needs_action else 0)


def report(args, name, problems):
    """Annotate each problem in GitHub format when asked; True when there is any."""
    if args.format == "github":
        for problem in problems:
            print("::error title=vibe-verifier consumers::%s: %s" % (name, problem))
    return bool(problems)


def gh_json(*api_args, method=None, fields=None, body=None):
    """A gh api call returning parsed JSON, with an optional JSON request body. Raises CannotRead with
    gh's own last line."""
    command = ["gh", "api", *api_args]
    if method:
        command += ["-X", method]
    for name, value in (fields or {}).items():
        command += ["-f", "%s=%s" % (name, value)]
    if body is not None:
        command += ["--input", "-"]
    result = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, input=None if body is None else json.dumps(body))
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise CannotRead((result.stderr.strip() or "gh api failed").splitlines()[-1])
    return json.loads(result.stdout) if result.stdout.strip() else {}


def gh_ok(endpoint):
    """True for an endpoint that answers 204, False for one that answers 404. Anything else is
    CannotRead: a switch that cannot be read must not report as off."""
    result = subprocess.run(["gh", "api", endpoint], capture_output=True, text=True)
    if result.returncode == 0:
        return True
    last = (result.stderr.strip() or "gh api failed").splitlines()[-1]
    if "HTTP 404" in last:
        return False
    raise CannotRead(last)


def bump_plan(args, target, wrapper=None):
    """([(repo, wrapper or None, new pin, [(path, [old pins], new text)])], [(repo, why skipped)]).

    Per repository, two levels: the catalog pins (a consumer's stub must match its template; a wrapper
    has no stub), moved to `target`; and the pins of the wrapper's workflows a caller names in a
    `uses:` line, moved to the wrapper's head (its repository names that level). Each level becomes
    its own pull request."""
    planned, skipped = [], []
    wrapper_head, wrapper_date = wrapper.head() if wrapper else (None, None)
    for repo in args._repos:
        is_wrapper = wrapper is not None and repo == wrapper.repo
        try:
            stub = gh_file(repo, STUB_PATH)
            state, sha = stub_status(stub)
            texts = workflow_texts(repo) if state == "ok" or wrapper else {}
        except CannotRead as error:
            skipped.append((repo, "could not read: %s" % error))
            continue
        calls = wrapper_calls(wrapper, texts) if wrapper and not is_wrapper else []
        catalog, wrapped = [], []
        if state == "ok" or is_wrapper:
            for path, text in sorted(texts.items()):
                moving = sorted({m.group(2) for m in PIN_LINE.finditer(text)
                                 if m.group(2) != target and not pin_status(args, m.group(2)).startswith("current")})
                if moving:
                    catalog.append((path, moving, PIN_LINE.sub(
                        lambda m: "%s%s # main %s" % (m.group(1), target, args._target_date) if m.group(2) in moving else m.group(0), text)))
        elif stub is not None or repo in (args.repo or []):
            skipped.append((repo, "stub %s" % state))
        elif not calls:
            continue  # discovery: a repository that never subscribed is not a finding
        for path in sorted({path for path, _, _ in calls}):
            moving = sorted({ref for called_from, called, ref in calls if called_from == path and ref != wrapper_head
                             and not wrapper.status(ref, called).startswith("current")})
            if moving:
                wrapped.append((path, moving, wrapper.call.sub(
                    lambda m: "%s%s # main %s" % (m.group(1), wrapper_head, wrapper_date) if m.group(3) in moving else m.group(0), texts[path])))
        if catalog:
            planned.append((repo, None, target, catalog))
        if wrapped:
            planned.append((repo, wrapper.repo, wrapper_head, wrapped))
        if not catalog and not wrapped and (state == "ok" or is_wrapper or calls):
            skipped.append((repo, "already pinned to %s" % target[:10] if sha == target else "pins are current"))
    return planned, skipped


def ruleset_plan(wrapper):
    """[(ruleset, [(workflow path, old pin)], new rules)] for every ruleset that pins a wrapper workflow
    anywhere but a current commit. Only those entries' `sha` changes, to the wrapper's head."""
    head, _ = wrapper.head()
    wrapper_id, found = wrapper_rulesets(wrapper)
    moves = []
    for ruleset, pins in found:
        moving = sorted({(path, old) for path, old in pins if old != head and wrapper.status(old, path) != "current"})
        if not moving:
            continue
        rules = copy.deepcopy(ruleset.get("rules") or [])
        for entry in required_workflows(rules):
            if entry.get("repository_id") == wrapper_id and (entry.get("path", ""), entry.get("sha") or entry.get("ref") or "") in moving:
                entry["sha"] = head
        moves.append((ruleset, moving, rules))
    return moves


def open_bump(repo, changes, target, branch, default_branch, wrapper=None):
    """One pull request changing only pin lines, one commit per workflow file. Returns its URL. With
    `wrapper` (OWNER/NAME) the pins are of that wrapper's workflows, not of catalog actions."""
    head = gh_json("repos/%s/git/ref/heads/%s" % (repo, default_branch))["object"]["sha"]
    gh_json("repos/%s/git/refs" % repo, method="POST", fields={"ref": "refs/heads/" + branch, "sha": head})
    what = "the %s workflow" % wrapper if wrapper else "the Vibe Verifier action"
    why = ("The workflow a caller runs comes from the pinned commit, so this is how a repository takes up the wrapper's changes."
           if wrapper else "The gates and actions a workflow runs come from the pinned commit, so this is how a repository takes up catalog changes.")
    for path, shas, text in changes:
        blob = gh_json("repos/%s/contents/%s?ref=%s" % (repo, path, default_branch))["sha"]
        message = "ci: bump %s pin to %s in %s\n\nPin lines only. %s\n" % (what, target[:10], path, why)
        gh_json("repos/%s/contents/%s" % (repo, path), method="PUT", fields={
            "message": message, "branch": branch, "sha": blob,
            "content": base64.b64encode(text.encode()).decode()})
    moved = "\n".join("- `%s`: %s -> `%s`" % (path, ", ".join("`%s`" % s[:10] for s in shas), target[:10]) for path, shas, _ in changes)
    proof = ("This PR's own run of the called workflow is the proof that it works here." if wrapper
             else "This PR's own `vibe-verifier-ok` run is the proof that the new catalog passes here.")
    result = subprocess.run(["gh", "pr", "create", "--repo", repo, "--base", default_branch, "--head", branch,
                             "--title", "ci: bump %s pin to %s" % (what, target[:10]),
                             "--body", "Moves %s pins to `%s`, and changes nothing else:\n\n%s\n\n%s %s\n\n"
                                       "## Acceptance criteria\n\n- None: this changes "
                                       "pinned commit SHAs in workflow files. No application code, route, or rendered surface "
                                       "changes, so there is nothing for a browser to check.\n\n"
                                       "🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)" % (
                                           "`%s` workflow" % wrapper if wrapper else "catalog", target[:10], moved, why, proof)],
                            capture_output=True, text=True)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise CannotRead((result.stderr.strip() or "gh pr create failed").splitlines()[-1])
    return result.stdout.strip().splitlines()[-1]


def apply_down(args):
    wrapper, error = load_wrapper(args)
    if error:
        return fail(error)
    try:
        discovered = set(owner_repos(args.owner)) - {source_repo(args)} if args.owner else set()
    except CannotRead as error:
        return fail(str(error))
    args._repos = sorted(set(args.repo or []) | discovered | ({wrapper.repo} if wrapper else set()))
    if not args._repos:
        return fail("no repositories given (use --repo OWNER/NAME or --owner OWNER)")
    resolved = source_git(args, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", args.to or args.source_ref)
    if resolved.returncode != 0:
        return fail("ref %s not found in %s" % (args.to or args.source_ref, args.source))
    target = resolved.stdout.strip()
    # The comment beside the pin says when the pin was taken; a bump that leaves the old date there
    # makes the line lie about the commit next to it.
    args._target_date = source_git(args, "log", "-1", "--date=short", "--format=%cd", target).stdout.strip()
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}", args._target_date):
        return fail("could not read the date of %s in %s" % (target[:10], args.source))
    planned, skipped = bump_plan(args, target, wrapper)
    moves = []
    if wrapper:
        try:
            moves = ruleset_plan(wrapper)
        except CannotRead as error:
            skipped.append(("rulesets of %s" % wrapper.owner, "could not read: %s" % error))
    for repo, reason in skipped:
        print("%-32s skipped: %s" % (repo, reason))
    if not planned and not moves:
        print("\nnothing to bump")
        return 2 if any("could not read" in reason for _, reason in skipped) else 0
    lines = [(repo, path, sha, to) for repo, _, to, changes in planned for path, shas, _ in changes for sha in shas]
    lines += [("ruleset %s/%s" % (wrapper.owner, ruleset.get("name")), path, old, wrapper.head()[0])
              for ruleset, moving, _ in moves for path, old in moving]
    digest = hashlib.sha256("\n".join("%s %s %s %s" % line for line in lines).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]
    for repo, path, sha, to in lines:
        print("%-32s %-40s %s -> %s" % (repo, path, sha[:10], to[:10]))
    if not args.confirm:
        print("\n%d pull request(s) and %d ruleset update(s) to make. Re-run with --confirm %s" % (len(planned), len(moves), digest))
        return 1
    if args.confirm != digest:
        return fail("plan digest is %s, not %s: the repositories moved since the plan, so read it again" % (digest, args.confirm))
    failed = False
    for repo, level, to, changes in planned:
        branch = "ci/%s-pin-%s" % ("wrapper" if level else "vibe-verifier", to[:10])
        try:
            default_branch = gh_json("repos/%s" % repo)["default_branch"]
            existing = gh_json("repos/%s/pulls?head=%s:%s&state=open" % (repo, repo.split("/")[0], branch))
            print("%-32s %s" % (repo, existing[0]["html_url"] if existing else open_bump(repo, changes, to, branch, default_branch, level)))
        except CannotRead as error:
            print("%-32s could not open: %s" % (repo, error))
            failed = True
    for ruleset, moving, rules in moves:
        name = "ruleset %s/%s" % (wrapper.owner, ruleset.get("name"))
        try:
            gh_json("orgs/%s/rulesets/%s" % (wrapper.owner, ruleset["id"]), method="PUT", body={"rules": rules})
            print("%-32s updated: %s" % (name, ", ".join(os.path.basename(path) for path, _ in moving)))
        except CannotRead as error:
            print("%-32s could not update: %s" % (name, error))
            failed = True
    return 2 if failed else 0


def tool_command(args):
    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(NODE_TOOLS, args.name, "package-lock.json")):
        return fail("no toolchain named %r under tools/ (available: %s)" % (
            args.name, ", ".join(sorted(n for n in os.listdir(NODE_TOOLS) if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(NODE_TOOLS, n, "package-lock.json")))) or "none"))
    try:
        print(ensure_node_tool(args.name))
    except CannotRun as error:
        return fail(str(error))
    return 0


def rules_doc_command(args):
    """Print or write the generated rules block for the rules the manifest's repo-rules lines enable."""
    try:
        enabled = repo_rules.manifest_rules(args.manifest)
        if not enabled:
            return fail("%s has no repo-rules line, so it enables no rules" % args.manifest)
        block = repo_rules.rules_doc(ensure("ast-grep"), repo_rules.head_sources(args.repo, *enabled))
    except CannotRun as error:
        return fail("rules-doc: %s" % error)
    if not args.write:
        print(block)
        return 0
    text = read_text(args.write) if os.path.isfile(args.write) else ""
    found = repo_rules.BLOCK.search(text)
    if found:
        text = text[:found.start()] + block + text[found.end():]
    else:
        text += ("\n" if text and not text.endswith("\n") else "") + ("\n" if text.strip() else "") + block + "\n"
    with open(args.write, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        handle.write(text)
    print("%s the rules block in %s" % ("replaced" if found else "appended", args.write))
    return 0


def read_text(path):
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
        return handle.read()


def criteria_command(args):
    if args.max_shards is not None and args.max_shards < 1:
        return fail("--max-shards is a whole number of explorers, 1 or more: %d" % args.max_shards)
    try:
        text = read_text(args.file)
        changed = [line for line in read_text(args.changed_files).splitlines() if line] if args.changed_files else None
        ticket_text = read_text(args.ticket) if args.ticket else ""
    except OSError as error:
        return fail("could not read %s" % error)
    reason = not_required(text, changed, ticket_text)
    if reason:
        print("none: %s" % reason)
        return 0
    ticket, problem = ticket_items(ticket_text, args.ticket)
    wanted = ([] if declares_none(text) else read_criteria(text)) + ticket
    print("criteria: %d" % len(wanted))
    if problem:
        print("ticket: %s" % problem)
    for item, wording in wanted:
        notes = annotations(wording)
        for label, values in (("references", notes.references), ("roles", notes.roles), ("malformed", notes.malformed)):
            if values:
                print("%s %s: %s" % (item, label, ", ".join(values)))
    if args.max_shards is not None:
        print("\n".join(shard_plan(wanted, manifest_widths(args), args.max_shards)))
    return 0


def manifest_widths(args):
    """The --widths on the manifest's qae-artifacts line, judged from the base; [] when it has none."""
    if not args.manifest:
        return []
    lines = [gate_args for gate, gate_args in judged_entries(args, notice=sys.stderr) if gate == "qae-artifacts"]
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="qae-artifacts", add_help=False)
    parser.add_argument("--widths", type=qae_artifacts.width_list, default=None)
    try:
        return (parser.parse_known_args(lines[0])[0].widths or []) if lines else []
    except SystemExit:
        sys.exit(fail("the qae-artifacts line in %s has a malformed --widths" % args.manifest))


# The Claude lane's turn cap (claude-code-action's --max-turns), sized to what the explorer walks: one walk is
# one criterion at one viewport width as one role, or up to three states of one feature's re-walk (three is the
# default --max-states; more states add walks in proportion, which is an assumption: no run has measured the
# turns one state takes). A fixed 80 cut off a correct run on
# a consumer (2026-10-07: two criteria at two widths took 54 turns, then more than 80 on a re-run of the same
# head). Its other runs took 18 and 34 turns for two criteria at one width, 39 for three, 44 for one criterion
# at two widths with a form, and 60 for two criteria at two widths. 40 plus 30 a walk is at least twice each of
# those, and twice the 80 the cut-off run stopped at. 80 stays the floor and 240 bounds it: at the slowest turn
# measured there (4.5 seconds) 240 turns end inside the explore job's 30 minutes.
TURNS_BASE, TURNS_PER_WALK, TURNS_FLOOR, TURNS_CEILING = 40, 30, 80, 240
STATES_PER_WALK = 3
# The walks one explorer is sized for: what the turn cap reaches before its ceiling, six. A consumer's pull
# request listed 13 broad criteria at two widths, at least 26 walks, for one explorer: it made one
# shallow pass, never signed in as two of the three roles it had logins for, stopped after 9 of its 30 minutes
# and FAILed 9 criteria as not completed. So the criteria of a pull request with more walks than this can be
# split across explorers that run in parallel (`criteria --max-shards`), each sized for this many.
SHARD_WALKS = (TURNS_CEILING - TURNS_BASE) // TURNS_PER_WALK
SHARE = ("Your share of the criteria is %s: you are explorer %d of %d. Walk only those, and write a verdict line for "
         "each of them and for no other criterion; the other criteria are walked by other explorers in parallel, "
         "which may use the same site, so expect data you did not create.")
# The first explorer when the feature re-walk is its whole load.
NO_SHARE = ("None of the criteria is yours: you are explorer %d of %d, and other explorers walk them in parallel, "
            "which may use the same site, so expect data you did not create. Walk no criterion and write no "
            "acceptance-check line. Re-walk the features as described below, and write only their lines in the verdict.")


def criterion_walks(wording, widths):
    """How many walks one criterion is: one as each role it names, at each of `widths`."""
    return max(1, len(annotations(wording).roles)) * max(1, len(widths))


def rewalk_walks(features, states):
    """How many walks the re-walk of `features` features is: one for each three states the explorer walks of one."""
    return features * -(-states // STATES_PER_WALK)


def explorer_turns(items, widths, features, states):
    """(walks, turn cap) for the criteria `items` ((id, wording) pairs) at each of `widths`, plus `features`
    re-walks of at most `states` states each."""
    walks = sum(criterion_walks(wording, widths) for _, wording in items) + rewalk_walks(features, states)
    return walks, min(TURNS_CEILING, max(TURNS_FLOOR, TURNS_BASE + TURNS_PER_WALK * walks))


def shard_shares(items, widths, shards, rewalk=0):
    """Each of `shards` explorers' share of the criteria `items`, in shard order. In document order, a criterion
    goes whole to the explorer with the fewest walks so far, the lowest-numbered on a tie. The first starts
    with `rewalk` walks, those of the feature re-walk only it does: a selected feature with no walked step is
    a finding, so the explorer that carries the re-walk takes fewer criteria and reaches it in its time. Every
    explore job makes this assignment for itself, from the same inputs, so they agree."""
    shares, loads = [[] for _ in range(shards)], [rewalk] + [0] * (shards - 1)
    for item in items:
        least = loads.index(min(loads))
        shares[least].append(item)
        loads[least] += criterion_walks(item[1], widths)
    return shares


def shard_plan(items, widths, max_shards):
    """What `criteria --max-shards` prints after the criteria. `shards: [1,2,3]` numbers the explorers, for the
    explore job's matrix: enough for SHARD_WALKS walks each, never more than `max_shards` nor than the criteria
    (one is never split), and at least one. Then the walks it counted, and a warning when they exceed what those
    explorers are sized for, so an oversized pull request shows up front. The count is of the criteria alone:
    which features are re-walked is selected in the explore job, from a checkout this job does not have."""
    walks = sum(criterion_walks(wording, widths) for _, wording in items)
    shards = max(1, min(max_shards, len(items), -(-walks // SHARD_WALKS)))
    lines = ["shards: [%s]" % ",".join(str(number) for number in range(1, shards + 1)), "walks: %d" % walks]
    if walks > shards * SHARD_WALKS:
        lines.append("::warning title=vibe-verifier criteria::%d walks (each criterion once per role and width) for %d "
                     "explorer%s sized for %d: expect criteria left unfinished. List fewer criteria on one pull request, "
                     "or raise max-shards (harnesses/qae/README.md)."
                     % (walks, shards, "" if shards == 1 else "s", shards * SHARD_WALKS))
    return lines


def explorer_share(items, widths, shard, shards, rewalk):
    """(the criteria explorer `shard` of `shards` walks, the sentence its prompt names them in). One explorer
    walks them all and is told nothing. One of several is told its share by code, never left to work it out.
    `rewalk` is the walks of the feature re-walk, the first explorer's alone."""
    if shards == 1:
        return items, ""
    share = shard_shares(items, widths, shards, rewalk)[shard - 1]
    return share, SHARE % (", ".join(item for item, _ in share), shard, shards) if share else NO_SHARE % (shard, shards)


def qae_inputs_command(args):
    """Copy each supplied design reference into the evidence, list it for the explorer, declare its digest,
    write the widths the manifest's qae-artifacts line requires and, asked, the explorer's turn cap and the
    sentence that names its share of the criteria."""
    if not 1 <= args.shard <= args.shards:
        return fail("--shard %d is not one of --shards %d" % (args.shard, args.shards))
    widths = manifest_widths(args)
    turns = None
    if args.turns:
        try:
            criteria_text = read_text(args.criteria)
            ticket_text = read_text(args.ticket) if os.path.isfile(args.ticket) else ""
        except OSError as error:
            return fail("could not read %s" % error)
        items = ([] if declares_none(criteria_text) else read_criteria(criteria_text)) + ticket_items(ticket_text)[0]
        names = os.listdir(args.features) if os.path.isdir(args.features) else []
        features = len([name for name in names if name.endswith(".md")])
        options = selection_options(args) if args.manifest and features else None
        states = options.max_states if options else STATES_PER_WALK
        items, share = explorer_share(items, widths, args.shard, args.shards, rewalk_walks(features, states))
        if args.shard > 1:
            # Every explorer counted the features, to share the criteria out alike. Only the first re-walks them.
            features = 0
            shutil.rmtree(args.features, ignore_errors=True)
            if os.path.isfile(args.features.rstrip("/") + ".md"):
                os.remove(args.features.rstrip("/") + ".md")
        if not items and not features and args.shards > 1:
            # The criteria job counted more criteria than this job reads: the body was edited between them.
            return fail("explorer %d of %d has no criterion to walk: the pull request's criteria changed after the "
                        "criteria job counted them, so run the workflow again" % (args.shard, args.shards))
        turns = explorer_turns(items, widths, features, states)
        with open(args.turns, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write("%d\n" % turns[1])
        if args.share:
            with open(args.share, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
                handle.write(share + "\n")
    if widths:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(args.widths_file) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        with open(args.widths_file, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write("".join("%d\n" % width for width in widths))
    names = sorted(os.listdir(args.references)) if os.path.isdir(args.references) else []
    images = [name for name in names if name.endswith(".png")]
    found = {}
    for name in images:
        path = os.path.join(args.references, name)
        size = png_size(path) if REFERENCE_KEY.match(name[:-4]) else None
        if not size:
            return fail("%s is not a PNG named <key>.png (a key is letters, digits, - and _)" % path)
        found[name[:-4]] = (path, size)
    digests = {}
    if found:
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(args.evidence, "references"), exist_ok=True)
        listing = ["# Design references", "",
                   "The workflow supplied these images. A criterion naming `[ref: <key>]` is compared with the "
                   "image of that key, at its width.", ""]
        for key, (path, (width, height)) in sorted(found.items()):
            shutil.copyfile(path, os.path.join(args.evidence, "references", key + ".png"))
            with open(path, "rb") as handle:
                digests[key] = hashlib.sha256(handle.read()).hexdigest()
            listing.append("- %s: %s, %d pixels wide (%d x %d)" % (key, path, width, width, height))
        with open(args.listing, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write("\n".join(listing) + "\n")
    if args.digests:
        with open(args.digests, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            handle.write(json.dumps(digests, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    print("\n".join(["references: %d" % len(found)] + ["%s: %dx%d" % (key, *size) for key, (_, size) in sorted(found.items())]
                    + ["widths: %s" % (", ".join(map(str, widths)) or "none")]
                    + (["max turns: %d (%d walks)" % (turns[1], turns[0])] if turns else [])
                    + (["share: %s (explorer %d of %d)" % (", ".join(item for item, _ in items) or "no criterion",
                                                           args.shard, args.shards)] if turns and args.shards > 1 else [])))
    return 0


def selection_options(args):
    """The selection options on the manifest's acceptance-verdict line, judged from the base; None when the
    manifest has no such line."""
    # The notice goes to stderr: a workflow reads the first line of `features` as `features: <n>`.
    lines = [gate_args for gate, gate_args in judged_entries(args, notice=sys.stderr) if gate == "acceptance-verdict"]
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="acceptance-verdict", add_help=False)
    add_selection_arguments(parser)
    try:
        return parser.parse_known_args(lines[0])[0] if lines else None
    except SystemExit:
        sys.exit(fail("the acceptance-verdict line in %s has a malformed selection option" % args.manifest))


def features_listing(chosen, states, out):
    """What the explorer reads beside the feature files in `out`: how many states to walk of each, and the
    changed paths that selected each, which say where in the feature to look first."""
    lines = ["# Features to re-walk", "",
             "Each feature below is described in %s/<id>.md, as this pull request has that file. Walk at most %d "
             "state%s of each, first the states closest to the changed files listed for it."
             % (out, states, "" if states == 1 else "s"), ""]
    return "\n".join(lines + ["- %s: %s" % (fid, ", ".join(paths)) for fid, paths in chosen.selected]) + "\n"


def features_command(args):
    """The features the acceptance-verdict line's selection picks for these changed paths."""
    options = selection_options(args)
    if not options or not options.features:
        print("features: 0 (the re-walk is off: %s has no acceptance-verdict line with --features)" % args.manifest)
        return 0
    options.changed_files = args.changed_files
    try:
        chosen, features = selection(args.repo, options, args.base_ref)
    except CannotRun as error:
        return fail("features: %s" % error)
    print("\n".join(["features: %d" % len(chosen.selected)] + describe(chosen)))
    if args.out and chosen.selected:
        os.makedirs(args.out, exist_ok=True)
        for fid, _ in chosen.selected:
            shutil.copyfile(os.path.join(args.repo, features[fid].path), os.path.join(args.out, fid + ".md"))
        if args.listing:
            with open(args.listing, "w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
                handle.write(features_listing(chosen, options.max_states, args.out))
    return 0


# The QAE's review comment: one per pull request, found again by this first line and by the identity a
# workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN posts as, so a comment anyone else writes is never edited.
REVIEW_MARKER = "<!-- vibe-verifier:qa-review -->"
REVIEW_AUTHOR = "github-actions[bot]"
REVIEW_HEADINGS = {
    "passed": "## QA review: passed \u2705",
    "changes-needed": "## QA review: changes needed \u274c",
    "not-required": "## QA review: not required \u2705",
    "could-not-run": "## QA review: could not run \u26a0\ufe0f",
}


EXPLORE_DONE = ("success", "skipped")


def review_state(args):
    """What the workflow concluded, in order: a pull request that needs no check, an explore job that
    did not finish, then the gates' own outcome. Gates that never ran (an earlier verify step failed)
    judged nothing, so that is could-not-run too, never a refusal. A skipped explore job found no
    criterion to walk: the verify job's first step fails when the criteria job that decided it did not
    finish, so its gates never run and that case lands on could-not-run too."""
    if args.not_required:
        return "not-required"
    if args.explore_result not in EXPLORE_DONE or args.gates_outcome not in ("success", "failure"):
        return "could-not-run"
    return "passed" if args.gates_outcome == "success" else "changes-needed"


def review_criteria(criteria_text, verdict_text, ticket_text=""):
    ticket, problem = ticket_items(ticket_text)
    wanted = ([] if declares_none(criteria_text) and (ticket or problem) else read_criteria(criteria_text)) + ticket
    rows = ["The ticket's criteria could not be read: %s." % problem] if problem else []
    if not wanted:
        return rows or ["The pull request lists no acceptance criteria. Add a `## Acceptance criteria` list, or "
                        "`- None: <why>` when nothing on the site changes."]
    lines = check_lines(verdict_text, "acceptance-check")
    for item, wording in wanted:
        source = " (from the ticket)" if item.startswith("TC") else ""
        rows.append("- %s%s, %s: %s" % (item, source, explorer_said(lines.get(item.casefold(), [])), wording))
    # A feature re-walk (docs/feature-map.md) adds one line per feature; which features were required is
    # the gate's to decide, so this lists what the explorer wrote and the gate's log names any missing.
    # A re-walk is partial by design, so the states the explorer says it left unwalked are listed too.
    skipped = check_lines(verdict_text, "regression-skip")
    for fid, carried in check_lines(verdict_text, "regression-check").items():
        unwalked = "; ".join(said(line, "regression-skip") for line in skipped.get(fid, []))
        rows.append("- Feature `%s` re-walked, %s%s" % (fid, explorer_said(carried), unwalked and " (not walked: %s)" % unwalked))
    return rows


def explorer_said(carried):
    return "no verdict" if len(carried) != 1 else "explorer says PASS" if verdict(carried[0]) == "PASS" else "explorer says FAIL"


def review_body(state, args):
    head = "`%s`" % args.head[:8]
    lines = [REVIEW_MARKER, REVIEW_HEADINGS[state], ""]
    if state == "not-required":
        lines.append("No browser check on %s: %s." % (head, args.not_required.rstrip(".")))
    elif state == "could-not-run":
        stopped = ("The explore job ended `%s`" % args.explore_result if args.explore_result not in EXPLORE_DONE
                   else "The verify job stopped before its gates ran")
        lines.append("%s on %s, so nothing was checked. Its log has the cause; rerun the workflow once it "
                     "is fixed." % (stopped, head))
    else:
        lines.append("Every criterion on %s has a PASS anchored to a step the explorer logged, and the "
                     "evidence gate found nothing." % head if state == "passed" else
                     "The QA gates refused %s. Their findings are in the qae-verify job's log." % head)
        lines.append("")
        verdict = read_text(args.verdict) if args.verdict and os.path.isfile(args.verdict) else ""
        ticket = read_text(args.ticket) if args.ticket and os.path.isfile(args.ticket) else ""
        lines += review_criteria(read_text(args.criteria), verdict, ticket)
    lines += ["", "[The run and its evidence](%s)" % args.run_url]
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def review_comment_ids(repo, pr):
    ids, page = [], 1
    while True:
        batch = gh_json("repos/%s/issues/%s/comments?per_page=100&page=%d" % (repo, pr, page))
        ids += [c["id"] for c in batch if c["user"]["login"] == REVIEW_AUTHOR and c["body"].startswith(REVIEW_MARKER)]
        if len(batch) < 100:
            return ids
        page += 1


def qa_review_command(args):
    try:
        state = review_state(args)
        body = review_body(state, args)
        found = review_comment_ids(args.repo, args.pr)
        if found:
            gh_json("repos/%s/issues/comments/%s" % (args.repo, found[0]), method="PATCH", body={"body": body})
        else:
            gh_json("repos/%s/issues/%s/comments" % (args.repo, args.pr), method="POST", body={"body": body})
    except (OSError, CannotRead) as error:
        return fail("could not post the QA review: %s" % error)
    print("%s the QA review: %s" % ("edited" if found else "posted", state))
    return 0


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="vibe-verifier", description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    commands = parser.add_subparsers(dest="command", required=True)
    runner = commands.add_parser("run", help="run the subscribed gates")
    runner.add_argument("--manifest", default=".vibe-verifier")
    runner.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    runner.add_argument("--base-ref", default=None)
    runner.add_argument("--format", choices=["text", "github"], default="text")
    commands.add_parser("list", help="list the gates in this catalog")
    inventory = commands.add_parser("consumers", help="inventory and drift check of subscribed repositories (read-only, via gh)")
    inventory.add_argument("--repo", action="append", metavar="OWNER/NAME", help="a repository to check (repeatable)")
    inventory.add_argument("--owner", metavar="OWNER", help="check every non-archived repository of this owner that subscribes")
    inventory.add_argument("--source", default=ROOT, help="a checkout of this repository, for pin ancestry (default: this one)")
    inventory.add_argument("--source-ref", default="origin/main", help="the ref pins are judged against (default: origin/main)")
    inventory.add_argument("--format", choices=["text", "github"], default="text")
    inventory.add_argument("--wrapper", metavar="PATH", help="a checkout of a wrapper repository (an organization's CI "
                           "repository whose workflows its rulesets require): inventory it, its rulesets and its callers")
    inventory.add_argument("--wrapper-ref", default="origin/main", help="the ref wrapper pins are judged against (default: origin/main)")
    bump = commands.add_parser("apply-down", help="open one pull request per consumer bumping a stale action pin")
    bump.add_argument("--repo", action="append", metavar="OWNER/NAME", help="a repository to bump (repeatable)")
    bump.add_argument("--owner", metavar="OWNER", help="bump every subscribed repository of this owner")
    bump.add_argument("--source", default=ROOT, help="a checkout of this repository (default: this one)")
    bump.add_argument("--source-ref", default="origin/main", help="the ref pins are judged against (default: origin/main)")
    bump.add_argument("--to", metavar="REF", default=None, help="pin to this ref instead of --source-ref")
    bump.add_argument("--confirm", metavar="DIGEST", default=None, help="the digest the plan printed; without it nothing is written")
    bump.add_argument("--wrapper", metavar="PATH", help="a checkout of a wrapper repository: bump its catalog pins, its "
                      "callers' pins of its workflows and its organization's ruleset pins")
    bump.add_argument("--wrapper-ref", default="origin/main", help="the wrapper ref pins move to (default: origin/main)")
    toolchain = commands.add_parser("tool", help="install (once per lockfile digest) and print the directory of a node toolchain under tools/")
    toolchain.add_argument("name", help="the toolchain: a directory under tools/ with a package-lock.json")
    reader = commands.add_parser("criteria", help="read a criteria document: prints `none: <reason>` or `criteria: <n>`")
    reader.add_argument("file", help="the PR body or criteria document")
    reader.add_argument("--changed-files", metavar="FILE", help="the pull request's changed paths, one per line")
    reader.add_argument("--ticket", metavar="FILE", help="the criteria of the ticket the pull request implements, "
                        "counted as TC1, TC2, ... in addition to its own (empty: no ticket)")
    reader.add_argument("--max-shards", metavar="N", type=int, default=None, help="also print `shards: [1,...]`, how "
                        "many explorers the criteria are split across: enough for six walks each, at most N")
    reader.add_argument("--manifest", metavar="FILE", help="with --max-shards, the QAE manifest: the --widths of its "
                        "qae-artifacts line, judged from the base, count toward the walks")
    reader.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    reader.add_argument("--base-ref", default=None)
    inputs = commands.add_parser("qae-inputs", help="copy the site step's design references into the QAE evidence, "
                                 "list them for the explorer and write their digests")
    inputs.add_argument("--references", default="qae-inputs/references", metavar="DIR",
                        help="where the site step wrote <key>.png (default: qae-inputs/references)")
    inputs.add_argument("--evidence", default="qae-artifacts", metavar="DIR",
                        help="the explorer's artifact directory; copies go to its references/ (default: qae-artifacts)")
    inputs.add_argument("--listing", default="qae-inputs/references.md", metavar="FILE",
                        help="the list the explorer reads (default: qae-inputs/references.md)")
    inputs.add_argument("--digests", metavar="FILE", help="write {key: sha256} here, one JSON object")
    inputs.add_argument("--manifest", metavar="FILE", help="the QAE manifest: its qae-artifacts line's --widths, judged "
                        "from the base, are written to --widths-file")
    inputs.add_argument("--widths-file", default="qae-inputs/widths", metavar="FILE",
                        help="where the widths go, one per line (default: qae-inputs/widths)")
    inputs.add_argument("--turns", metavar="FILE", help="write the explorer's turn cap here, sized to its walks")
    inputs.add_argument("--criteria", default="qae-inputs/pr-body.md", metavar="FILE",
                        help="with --turns, the PR body whose criteria are walked (default: qae-inputs/pr-body.md)")
    inputs.add_argument("--ticket", default="qae-inputs/ticket.md", metavar="FILE",
                        help="with --turns, the ticket's criteria, when the file exists (default: qae-inputs/ticket.md)")
    inputs.add_argument("--features", default="qae-inputs/features", metavar="DIR",
                        help="with --turns, the features to re-walk, one <id>.md each (default: qae-inputs/features); "
                        "counted by every explorer, and removed, with its listing DIR.md, for one past the first")
    inputs.add_argument("--shard", metavar="K", type=int, default=1,
                        help="with --turns, which explorer of --shards this is: the cap is sized to its share (default: 1)")
    inputs.add_argument("--shards", metavar="N", type=int, default=1,
                        help="with --turns, how many explorers the criteria are split across (default: 1)")
    inputs.add_argument("--share", metavar="FILE", help="with --turns, write the sentence that names this explorer's "
                        "share of the criteria here, for its prompt (an empty line for one explorer)")
    inputs.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    inputs.add_argument("--base-ref", default=None)
    rewalk = commands.add_parser("features", help="the features a pull request's changed paths touch, by the selection "
                                 "on the manifest's acceptance-verdict line: prints `features: <n>`")
    rewalk.add_argument("--manifest", default=".vibe-verifier-qae")
    rewalk.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    rewalk.add_argument("--base-ref", default=None)
    rewalk.add_argument("--changed-files", required=True, metavar="FILE", help="the pull request's changed paths, one per line")
    rewalk.add_argument("--out", metavar="DIR", help="copy each selected feature file here as <id>.md")
    rewalk.add_argument("--listing", metavar="FILE", help="with --out, write the list the explorer reads here: the most "
                        "states to walk of each feature and the changed paths that selected each")
    doc = commands.add_parser("rules-doc", help="print, or write into a file, the generated block listing the rules "
                              "the manifest's repo-rules lines enable")
    doc.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    doc.add_argument("--manifest", default=".vibe-verifier")
    doc.add_argument("--write", metavar="PATH", help="replace the block in this file, or append it when the file has none")
    review = commands.add_parser("qa-review", help="post or edit the QAE's one review comment on a pull request (via gh)")
    review.add_argument("--repo", required=True, metavar="OWNER/NAME")
    review.add_argument("--pr", required=True, metavar="N")
    review.add_argument("--head", required=True, metavar="SHA", help="the head commit the run judged")
    review.add_argument("--run-url", required=True, metavar="URL")
    review.add_argument("--criteria", required=True, metavar="FILE", help="the PR body the run read")
    review.add_argument("--verdict", metavar="FILE", help="the explorers' verdict, as the verify job read it from "
                        "the run's evidence, when there is one")
    review.add_argument("--ticket", metavar="FILE", help="the ticket's criteria the run read, when the workflow supplies them")
    review.add_argument("--explore-result", required=True, help="the explore job's result (success, skipped, failure, cancelled)")
    review.add_argument("--gates-outcome", default="", help="the gates step's outcome (success, failure, skipped)")
    review.add_argument("--not-required", default="", metavar="REASON", help="why no browser check is needed; empty when one is")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    if args.command == "list":
        print("\n".join(available()))
        return 0
    if args.command == "consumers":
        return consumers(args)
    if args.command == "apply-down":
        return apply_down(args)
    if args.command == "criteria":
        return criteria_command(args)
    if args.command == "features":
        return features_command(args)
    if args.command == "qae-inputs":
        return qae_inputs_command(args)
    if args.command == "qa-review":
        return qa_review_command(args)
    if args.command == "tool":
        return tool_command(args)
    if args.command == "rules-doc":
        return rules_doc_command(args)
    return run(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
